{
  "reviews": [
    {
      "actor": "lin",
      "decision": "develop",
      "ability": null,
      "reason": "Bark and vine are already registered materials, but registration does not establish an observed, accessible supply. Gather only collects exposed material; it does not strip bark or manufacture binding. The observed willows provide a feasible precursor: remove a finite willow-stock portion, separate usable bark and bast, and twist the bast into a real flexible binding. This requires new processing rather than new stock, automatic warmth, or replacement protection recipes.",
      "specification": "Implement willow-materials@1 with interface process-willow. Public schema: object with mode enum [strip,twist], target string maxLength 1000, additionalProperties false; both fields required. Use shared object:ID/item:ID/resource:ID references, compatibility aliases and ambiguity reporting; strip resolves a visible willow object, twist resolves owned willow-bast. Resolve identities once per operation. Learning: 4 rounds, covering hand separation, safe partial harvesting and cord construction; no prior ability prerequisite. Dependencies: shared inventory/reservation, source-stock, geometry, material provenance, work-progress and event systems; existing mantle/hearth handlers must accept materially qualified willow bark and bast cord, not merely names.\nState and executable precursor: strip removes one actual willow resource unit from a reachable standing willow stock, representing a hand-bendable shoot bundle rather than felling the trunk. Require stock>=1 and an unobstructed worker position within 0.65 of its collision surface; never allow standing inside the trunk or interaction through walls. Four stationary rounds cost 2 energy each plus normal metabolism. Reserve and debit the source unit atomically at start into actor-owned work escrow, recording source identity, harvested quantity, moisture and mass. Success consumes its 1.2 mass and produces bark quantity2/mass0.6, willow-bast quantity1/mass0.5, and willow-offcut quantity1/mass0.1. No extra wood is produced. The tree remains solid and its actual stock falls by one; do not add regrowth or a second bark stock.\nTwist reserves one owned willow-bast unit and takes three stationary rounds at 1 energy each. Success transforms its entire 0.5 mass into one owned bast-cord unit. Declare bast-cord a physically qualified vine-equivalent binding: flexibility9, fuel2, moisture inherited from its input. Bark inherits willow moisture and supplies flexible panels; preserve its willow provenance rather than presenting it as birch. Offcut is nonedible, mass0.1, with fuel value derived proportionally from the consumed wood. All outputs retain input lineage; none supplies food or nutrition. Existing recipe selectors accept explicit bast-cord references wherever vine binding is required, without double-counting reservations or weakening their costs. Output moisture continues to affect applicable downstream fire and insulation mechanisms.\nExecution contract: show paid rounds and actual remaining work after each step; completion occurs exactly once after all required rounds and valid conditions. Insufficient energy, lost reach/visibility for strip, movement, changed action, invalid target or death cancels. Unprocessed escrow remains identifiable material, not restored standing stock: release the surviving shoot bundle or bast once to the living owner's inventory, or at the death location. Permit a strip restart using that owned shoot bundle, with full remaining processing work disclosed; spent effort is never refunded. No cancellation clones, deletes or resets material age. Global pauses preserve escrow and progress without advancing time. Repeats count completed operations; ordinary metabolism and environmental exposure continue during actual work.\nMigration: register material definitions and processing metadata without spawning items or sources. Existing willow stock is the sole harvest budget; old saves gain no additional stock. Preserve all existing learned abilities, histories, recipes and mental accounts. Material selectors, inspection, inventory, task adapters and replay must expose exact provenance, binding compatibility, work status and moisture. Implementation, personal learning and subsequent use remain separate.",
      "experienceReview": "Material access has no direct warming, health or happiness effect. Actual wearing or fire use may later qualify for the existing shared thermal-refuge source under its measured conditions, never a duplicate source. Successful hands-on processing can also provide modest personal tactile engagement, not an achievement reward. Declare willow-engagement-qualified for the performing actor only: a completed strip or twist must include at least three paid, uninterrupted attentive rounds, successful usable output, satiety>=20 throughout and no health loss. This event grants transient engagement 1, cap1, for 6 rounds, with a shared 12-round cooldown across both modes. Repetition remains eligible without novelty. Separately, qualifying paid processing rounds gradually maintain the same engagement source at 0.025 per round, cap1; absent qualifying participation it decays 0.003 per round. Use max(transient,sustained), not their sum, within the global mental cap. Cancelled work, requests, learning, labels and mere possession do not qualify. Do not modify protected mental accounts or nutrition.",
      "visualReview": "Declare subscription-valid events willow-work-started, willow-work-progress, willow-work-completed, willow-work-cancelled and willow-engagement-qualified. Replay records resolved actor/source/input/output identities, position, paid energy, reserved and consumed mass, stock changes, remaining work and cancellation disposition. Show hand stripping or twisting only during paid work, a finite source-stock change, and distinct bark panels, bast fibres, cord and offcuts after completion. Inspection must distinguish willow-derived bark from birch bark and identify cord as a manufactured binding. No hearth, mantle, warmth aura or completed product appears before its actual paid creation.",
      "learning": 4
    }
  ]
}
